::ALG1_U06_L01_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
The graphs of y = x + 3 and y = -x + 7 intersect at (2, 5). What is the solution?
{
=A. (2, 5)#The solution is the intersection point.
~B. (5, 2)#This reverses the coordinates.
~C. x = 2#This gives only one coordinate.
~D. y = 5#This gives only one coordinate.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
Which point satisfies both y = x + 1 and y = 2x - 2?
{
~A. (1, 2)#This works in y = x + 1 but not in y = 2x - 2.
~B. (2, 4)#This works in y = 2x - 2 but not in y = x + 1.
=C. (3, 4)#4 = 3 + 1 and 4 = 2(3) - 2.
~D. (4, 3)#This reverses the coordinates.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
What is true about y = 4x + 2 and y = 4x - 6?
{
~A. The system has one solution#Same slope and different intercepts means the lines do not cross.
=B. The system has no solution#The lines are parallel.
~C. The system has infinitely many solutions#The lines are not identical because the intercepts differ.
~D. The solution is (0, 2)#That point is only on the first line.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
Two equations graph as the same line. How many solutions does the system have?
{
~A. No solution#No solution happens with parallel distinct lines.
~B. Exactly one solution#One solution happens when lines cross once.
~C. Exactly two solutions#Two different lines cannot intersect at exactly two points.
=D. Infinitely many solutions#Every point on the shared line satisfies both equations.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
Which ordered pair is the solution to the system y = -x + 8 and y = x + 2?
{
=A. (3, 5)#Both equations give y = 5 when x = 3.
~B. (5, 3)#This reverses the coordinates and does not satisfy both equations.
~C. (0, 8)#This is the y-intercept of one line only.
~D. (0, 2)#This is the y-intercept of one line only.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q06::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
On a graph, two lines cross at (-1, 4). What should be reported as the solution?
{
~A. x = -1#This is only the x-coordinate.
=B. (-1, 4)#The solution must include both coordinates.
~C. (4, -1)#This reverses x and y.
~D. y = 4#This is only the y-coordinate.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q07::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
Which point satisfies both y = 3x - 1 and y = x + 5?
{
~A. (1, 2)#This works in y = 3x - 1 but not in y = x + 5.
~B. (2, 7)#This works in y = x + 5 but not in y = 3x - 1.
~C. (8, 3)#This reverses the coordinates.
=D. (3, 8)#8 = 3(3) - 1 and 8 = 3 + 5.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q08::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
Two lines have slopes 2 and -1. What does that tell you before graphing?
{
~A. The lines must be parallel#Parallel lines have equal slopes.
~B. The system has no solution#Different slopes mean the lines will intersect once.
~C. The equations are identical#Identical lines have the same slope and intercept.
=D. The system has one solution#Lines with different slopes intersect at exactly one point.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q09::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
Which system has no solution?
{
=A. y = 2x + 1 and y = 2x - 3#Same slope and different intercepts create parallel lines.
~B. y = 2x + 1 and y = -2x + 1#Different slopes create one intersection.
~C. y = x and y = x#These are identical and have infinitely many solutions.
~D. y = x + 1 and y = 3x - 5#Different slopes create one solution.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q10::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
Which system has infinitely many solutions?
{
~A. y = x + 4 and y = -x + 4#Different slopes create one solution.
~B. y = 5x - 2 and y = 5x + 3#Same slope but different intercepts creates no solution.
~C. y = -3x and y = 3x#Different slopes create one solution.
=D. y = 2x + 6 and 2y = 4x + 12#The second equation simplifies to y = 2x + 6.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q11::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
The point (2, 7) lies on both lines in a system. What does that mean?
{
~A. The system has no solution#A shared point is a solution.
=B. (2, 7) is the solution#A point on both lines satisfies both equations.
~C. The point is only the y-intercept#A point with x = 2 is not a y-intercept.
~D. The slopes must be equal#Lines can share one point and have different slopes.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q12::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
Which ordered pair satisfies y = -2x + 9 and y = x?
{
~A. (1, 1)#This gives -2(1) + 9 = 7, not 1.
~B. (2, 2)#This gives -2(2) + 9 = 5, not 2.
=C. (3, 3)#-2(3) + 9 = 3.
~D. (4, 4)#This gives -2(4) + 9 = 1, not 4.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q13::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
A system's graph shows parallel lines. Which statement is true?
{
=A. There is no ordered pair that satisfies both equations#Parallel lines do not intersect.
~B. There is exactly one solution#One solution requires one intersection.
~C. Every point on either line is a solution#That only happens when the lines are identical.
~D. The solution is the y-intercept#The y-intercepts are different points on different lines.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q14::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
Which point is on both y = x - 2 and y = -x + 4?
{
~A. (0, -2)#This is only on y = x - 2.
~B. (1, 3)#This reverses the solution coordinates.
=C. (3, 1)#1 = 3 - 2 and 1 = -3 + 4.
~D. (4, 0)#This is only on y = -x + 4.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q15::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
What should you do after reading an intersection point from a graph?
{
~A. Use only the x-coordinate as the answer#A system solution needs both coordinates.
~B. Round both coordinates automatically#Rounding is not needed unless the graph is approximate.
~C. Change both equations to have the same slope#Changing the equations changes the system.
=D. Check the ordered pair in both equations#Checking confirms the point satisfies the system.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q16::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
Two gym plans are y = 20 + 10x and y = 50 + 5x. The graphs intersect at (6, 80). What does 6 represent?
{
~A. The total cost in dollars#The y-coordinate gives the total cost.
=B. The number of months when the costs are equal#The x-coordinate represents months.
~C. The monthly fee for both plans#The equations have different monthly rates.
~D. The starting cost#The starting costs are the y-intercepts, 20 and 50.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q17::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
Which ordered pair satisfies both y = 4x and y = x + 6?
{
=A. (2, 8)#8 = 4(2) and 8 = 2 + 6.
~B. (8, 2)#This reverses the coordinates.
~C. (0, 6)#This is on y = x + 6 but not on y = 4x.
~D. (1, 4)#This is on y = 4x but not on y = x + 6.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q18::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
Which statement best describes the solution of y = -x + 3 and y = -x + 3?
{
~A. No solution#The equations are the same line, not parallel distinct lines.
=B. Infinitely many solutions#Every point on the line satisfies both equations.
~C. One solution at (0, 3)#That point is one solution, but not the only one.
~D. One solution at (3, 0)#That point is one solution, but not the only one.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q19::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
Which system has one solution?
{
~A. y = 2x + 1 and y = 2x + 1#Identical lines have infinitely many solutions.
~B. y = -3x + 4 and y = -3x - 2#Same slope and different intercepts have no solution.
=C. y = x + 5 and y = -2x - 1#Different slopes create exactly one intersection.
~D. y = 4x and y = 4x#Identical lines have infinitely many solutions.
}


::ALG1_U06_L01_Q20::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
The graphs of two equations intersect at (0, -2). Which statement is true?
{
~A. x = -2 and y = 0#This reverses the coordinates.
~B. Only one equation must contain the point#A system solution must satisfy both equations.
=C. x = 0 and y = -2 satisfy both equations#The intersection point belongs to both lines.
~D. The lines are parallel#Parallel lines do not intersect.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q21::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
What does the intersection point of two lines in a system represent?
{
=A. The solution that satisfies both equations #The intersection is the ordered pair that makes both equations true.
~B. The y-intercept of only the first line #An intersection may not be a y-intercept.
~C. The slope of both lines #A point is not the same as slope.
~D. A value that satisfies neither equation #The intersection satisfies both equations.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q22::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
A system has lines that cross at (2, 5). What is the solution?
{
~A. (5, 2) #This reverses the x- and y-values.
=B. (2, 5) #The solution of a graphed system is the intersection point.
~C. 2 only #A system solution is an ordered pair.
~D. 5 only #A system solution is an ordered pair.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q23::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
What kind of system has no solution when graphed?
{
~A. Intersecting lines #Intersecting lines have one solution.
~B. The same line graphed twice #The same line has infinitely many solutions.
~C. A horizontal and vertical line crossing #Crossing lines have one solution.
=D. Parallel lines #Distinct parallel lines never intersect.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q24::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
What kind of system has infinitely many solutions?
{
~A. Distinct parallel lines #Distinct parallel lines have no solution.
~B. Two lines crossing once #Crossing lines have one solution.
=C. The same line represented by both equations #Every point on the shared line satisfies both equations.
~D. Two unrelated points #A system of lines is not solved by unrelated points.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q25::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5
Question:
Why should a graphed solution be checked in both equations?
{
=A. The solution must satisfy both equations in the system #Checking confirms the ordered pair works for both relationships.
~B. Only one equation matters #A system requires both equations.
~C. Checking changes the graph #Checking verifies the solution; it does not change the graph.
~D. Ordered pairs cannot be substituted #Ordered pairs can be substituted into equations.
}
